ecm: fix NSS VLAN tags for Wi-Fi flows over a VLAN-aware DSA bridge

With br-lan vlan_filtering=1 on the RD03v2 (AN8855, tag_8021q tagger, CPU
port a tagged member of every bridge VLAN per 999-2762), accelerated flows
between a Wi-Fi VAP and anything else stalled as soon as ECM pushed the rule,
because the rule's VLAN tags did not match the frames on either side. With
ECM's IPv4 front end stopped, the same flows passed.

1. Bridged DSA port <-> VAP: no tag on the conduit side.

   ecm_nss_common_dsa_8021q_vid_get() returns 0 for a port of a VLAN-aware
   bridge, and the NSS frontend never reads ECM's bridge VLAN filter data, so
   the rule carried no VLAN. Frames from the switch arrive on eth1 tagged with
   the bridge VID; the NSS forwarded them to the VAP with a 4-byte shifted
   Ethernet header (seen on the client: "59:90:81:00:00:14 >
   37:df:00:e0:4c:12" for 00:e0:4c:12:59:90 > 00:05:16:5d:37:df on VLAN 20).
   Clients drop that unless the shifted DA happens to be multicast: a BCM43455
   and an RT3070 received 0 of ~9000 UDP frames on VLAN 1 and VLAN 20, a
   QCA9377 received all of them by luck. Frames towards the switch left the
   NSS untagged and fell into the CPU port's all-ports PVID.

   For a tag_8021q DSA user port of a VLAN-aware bridge, take the VLAN ECM
   recorded for that port in ci->vlan_filter, whatever the port's untagged
   flag, and only when both directions recorded the same VID for the port
   (flow ingress/return egress for the 'from' side, flow egress/return
   ingress for the 'to' side). ecm_db_connection_add_vlan_filter() carries
   one side's VID into the other side's walk and br_fdb_find_vid_by_mac()
   returns the newest FDB entry for a MAC in any VLAN, so the entries can disagree
   (a routed flow through the bridge master to a trunk port, a host using
   one MAC on several VLANs); such a connection is refused acceleration
   instead of getting a wrong or missing tag.

2. Routed VAP <-> WAN over br-lan.N: a tag on the Wi-Fi side.

   The stock VLAN branch copies the br-lan.N VID into the rule for the whole
   side, but the side's innermost interface is the VAP, an untagged bridge
   member that never sees the tag. Drop the primary tag of a side when it
   comes from a VLAN netdev on a VLAN-filtering bridge and the innermost
   interface is a non-DSA bridge port that is untagged in that VLAN. DSA
   ports keep the tag: their conduit carries it.

Measured with this patch (RD03v2, K1 image, br-lan VLAN 1 untagged lan2-4,
VLAN 20 tagged, rd03v2-iot SSID on VLAN 20):
  bridged UDP wired->Wi-Fi: BCM43455 3810/3810, RT3070 3622/3806 (2.4 GHz),
  QCA9377 3809/3809, correct headers; TCP both directions accelerated
  (85/87, 7-12, 68/214 Mbit/s); Wi-Fi->wired frames leave untagged on VLAN 1
  and tagged on VLAN 20.

Not covered: non-ported and multicast rules; routed flows between two VLANs
of the same bridge are still refused by ecm_db_connection_add_vlan_filter()
(slow path, correct), as are bridged flows to a host that uses one MAC on
several VLANs of the bridge.

